-- title:
--   Rank substitution on block cubes
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Let $I$ be an $m\times r$ matrix over $\mathbb F_2$.
--
--   1. **Block coordinates.** A point $\zeta\in\{0,1\}^{rk}$ is read as $k$ vectors in $\mathbb F_2^r$.
--   2. **Rank substitution.** The $m$ cube points in $\{0,1\}^k$ given by
--   $$
--   u_i(p)=\bigl(I\,\zeta^{(p)}\bigr)_i ,
--   $$
--   which realize Walsh patterns of rank at most $r$ by linear substitution. Averages over $\zeta$ turn rank-$r$ frequencies into products of lower-dimensional Walsh sums.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/Cotype/RankWalsh.lean

